; See pos-dup.lara: its single claim block is declared twice. This is a
; `pre-boundary` anchor -- the map never reaches the checked boundary, so no
; envelope exists and there is nothing to hand the Lean driver. What the harness
; compares is that both Haskell doors agree the map never got that far.
;
; The policy is copied in beside the member rather than shared with
; ../agreement/, because a member resolves its policy CO-LOCATED with itself: a
; parent-relative member would look for the policy next to itself and not find
; it, and the map would then be refused for an unreadable policy instead of for
; the duplicate claim -- the same exit code for the wrong reason, which the
; conformance harness could not tell apart.
(lara-map@1
  (policy empirical-v1 empirical-v1.policy.lara)
  (backends (backend nd 1))
  (members
    (member paper_pos pos-dup.lara)
    (member paper_neg ../agreement/neg.lara))
  (alignments)
  (questions))
